\title{One plain order sent to one broker, with nothing watching it afterwards}
\description{
This is what UBI's order engine runs when an order carries no \code{synthetic} object at all, unless the order is a plain limit or market order that UBI holds or follows the book with. Asking for it by name is useful for four things: to send a \code{limit} order to the broker at once, since a plain limit order with a price is otherwise held inside UBI as a \code{virtual_limit} until the other side reaches its price; to send a real \code{market} order, since a plain market order is otherwise run as a \code{marketable_limit} that follows the other side of the book for 30 seconds and is refused with HTTP 409 when that side is empty; to mark the order as closing a position with \code{closes_position}, so it may use the share of a broker's daily order cap kept for exits; and to make it reduce-only with \code{reduce_only}.

The order template's attributes are described on \code{SyntheticOrder}, and this type adds none of its own.
}
